        "id": "ch7_4_2-2",
        "text": "an API to update the information on a server.\nLike ConvertFrom-Json , ConvertTo-Json is a deceptively simple cmdlet that hides a lot of\nwork behind a short list of parameters. Let\u2019s take a look at them:\n\u2022 -AsArray will convert a PSObject into a JSON array unconditionally. Consider the following\nexamples in the following figure.\nFigure 7.17 \u2013 The -AsArray parameter\nPowerShell and the Web \u2013 HTTP, REST, and JSON\n186\n\u2022 In the first line, I\u2019m converting two strings into JSON. ConvertTo-Json automatically treats\nthem as an array and puts them in square brackets because they are two separate objects. In the\nsecond line, I\u2019m only feeding a single string into the pipeline, and ConvertTo-Json treats\nit as a single string. But what if I want it to be formatted as a single-member array? Then, I use\nthe -AsArray parameter, and I get my square brackets.\n\u2022 -Compress removes whitespace and indented formatting from the JSON output. The output\nwill be on a single line.\n\u2022 -Depth specifies how many levels of nested objects can be included in the JSON output. We\ncan have between zero and one hundred levels of nesting, but the default is a miserly two levels,\nwhich can catch people out. We get a warning if our output has more than this level. Consider\nthe JSON in the following figure:\nFigure 7.18 \u2013 nesting.json\nIf we ingest it into a variable, then convert it back into JSON, and then we get the warning in the\nfollowing figure. Notice that the third level of nesting, on the line beginning with \"really?\" ,\nis not converted into a JSON object, but into a string representing a hashtable:\nFigure 7.19 \u2013 We need to increase -Depth\nWorking with JSON\n187\n\u2022 -EnumsAsStrings will convert all the values of the enum type in the input object into",
